namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup regions

\section regions-intro Introduction

\section regions-slicing Slices and patches

Images and sequences feature slicing and patching capabilities that allow for efficient manipulation of subregions.
Slices are used to create subregion views as instances of `Image` or `Sequence`.
Therefore, slices have have a strided memory and their domains are necessarily regular, i.e., 1D intervals or ND axis-aligned boxes.
Slices are created with a syntax analogous to Python's slicing:

- `Slice()` means to select all elements along a dimension;
- `Slice(index)` selects a single index;
- `Slice(start, stop, step)` selects elements from index `start` to index `stop - 1`.

Such constructs can be combined to create multi-dimensional slices.

```cpp
// Keep x from 1 to 3, all y's, and z = 5
// The output slice is a 2D Image
auto slice = image[Linx::Slice(1, 4)()(5)];
assert(slice.n == 2);
assert(slice.domain() == Linx::Box<2>({0, 0}, {3, image.extent(1)}));
assert(slice.data() == image.data());
```

In contrast to slices, no assumptions are made on the memory layout of patches, therefore they support domains of any kind.
`Patch` is an extremely lightweight class that simply holds a domain and a pointer to the parent data.

```cpp
auto patch = Linx::where(image, any_domain);
assert(patch.domain() == any_domain);
assert(patch.parent() == image);
assert(patch.data() == image.data());
```

Still, simple domains like boxes or slices can be used to create patches as well:

```cpp
auto patch = Linx::where(image, Linx::Slice(1, 4)()(5));
assert(patch.n == 3);
```

*/

}
